    {
      "text": "The Saar region was placed under League of Nations administration with French coal rights, while the Rhineland was demilitarized, barring German troops or fortifications",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "saar"
    },
    {
      "text": "Stabilization via the Rentenmark in late 1923 and the Dawes Plan in 1924 reduced payments and secured U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "stabilization"
    },
    {
      "text": "The downturn spread globally via interconnected trade and finance, as American demand for imports evaporated and nations raised tariffs, such as the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "downturn"
    },
    {
      "text": "Smoot-Hawley Tariff Act of 1930, which deepened the contraction by reducing world trade by two-thirds",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "smoot"
    },
    {
      "text": "By 1933, the crisis had inflicted severe damage: U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "crisis"
    },
    {
      "text": "Globally, output in industrialized nations fell sharply, with industrial production in Germany and the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "output"
    },
    {
      "text": "These measures mitigated some collapse but sustained stagnation, reinforcing Mussolini's authoritarian control by associating economic woes with foreign dependencies and justifying aggressive foreign policy",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "these"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Depression's pervasive hardship\u2014marked by breadlines, farm foreclosures, and industrial idle capacity\u2014undermined liberal democracies and international cooperation, fostering isolationism in the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "depression"
    },
    {
      "text": "Nazi ideology fused nationalism, eugenics, and anti-Bolshevism, promising economic revival through rearmament and autarky",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "ideology"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Great Purge (1936-1938) executed or imprisoned over 700,000, including Bolshevik old guard, to eliminate dissent, consolidating Stalin's personal rule and preparing for militarized socialism",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "great"
    },
    {
      "text": "Unlike fascist variants, Soviet ideology claimed scientific inevitability of communism, yet mirrored them in terror and atomization of society to prevent organic opposition",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "unlike"
    },
    {
      "text": "This authoritarian surge, rooted in Meiji-era expansionism, paralleled totalitarian aggression by subordinating politics to military imperatives",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "surge"
    },
    {
      "text": "This event integrated Austria's resources and population into the Reich, enhancing Germany's military capacity without cost, and underscored appeasement's pattern of rewarding aggression",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "event"
    },
    {
      "text": "This policy's collapse shifted Europe toward confrontation, highlighting the perils of decoupling diplomacy from credible military power",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "s"
    },
    {
      "text": "The invasions marked the effective end of Polish sovereignty, with the country partitioned and subjected to immediate deportations, executions, and administrative restructuring by both occupiers",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "invasions"
    },
    {
      "text": "The treaty's harsh terms displaced over 400,000 Finnish Karelians, fueling resentment that contributed to Finland's later alignment with Germany in 1941",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "treaty"
    },
    {
      "text": "The war's outcome humiliated Stalin, prompting military reforms, but underscored Soviet willingness to use force for territorial gains",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "outcome"
    },
    {
      "text": "The operation disregarded the 1939 Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact, which Hitler viewed as a temporary expedient to avoid a two-front war while conquering Western Europe",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "operation"
    },
    {
      "text": "Stalin initially ordered no retaliation, fearing provocation, allowing German forces to achieve tactical surprise and penetrate border defenses rapidly",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "initially"
    },
    {
      "text": "The front's scale dwarfed Western campaigns, stretching German logistics and initiating a war of attrition that negated blitzkrieg assumptions",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "front"
    },
    {
      "text": "Japanese leaders, facing resource shortages, viewed a preemptive strike on the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "leaders"
    },
    {
      "text": "The attack involved 353 aircraft in two waves: the first targeting airfields and battleships with torpedoes, dive bombers, and fighters starting at 7:55 a",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_War_II",
      "agent": "attack"
    },
