    },
    {
      "text": "An American edition followed in 1899 from Doubleday & McClure Company, after the novel had been serialized in U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Dracula",
      "agent": "edition"
    },
    {
      "text": "An American edition followed in 1899 from Doubleday & McClure Company, after the novel had been serialized in U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Dracula",
      "agent": "edition"
    },
    {
      "text": "Comparative analysis confirms both as unauthorized derivations, likely enabled by weak international copyright enforcement at the time, rather than faithful translations",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Dracula",
      "agent": "confirms"
    },
    {
      "text": "Comparative analysis confirms both as unauthorized derivations, likely enabled by weak international copyright enforcement at the time, rather than faithful translations",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Dracula",
      "agent": "confirms"
    },
    {
      "text": "Early American printings, such as the 1899 Doubleday & McClure edition, followed the British text closely without abridgment, though some subsequent U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Dracula",
      "agent": "edition"
    },
    {
      "text": "Early American printings, such as the 1899 Doubleday & McClure edition, followed the British text closely without abridgment, though some subsequent U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Dracula",
      "agent": "edition"
    },
    {
      "text": "The 1897 Constable edition thus retains primacy for scholarly and interpretive purposes, with variants valued mainly for historical insight into adaptation practices",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Dracula",
      "agent": "edition"
    },
    {
      "text": "The 1897 Constable edition thus retains primacy for scholarly and interpretive purposes, with variants valued mainly for historical insight into adaptation practices",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Dracula",
      "agent": "edition"
    },
    {
      "text": "This compilation emulates forensic or archival evidence, fostering verisimilitude by framing implausible supernatural occurrences as aggregated firsthand testimonies subject to cross-verification, rather than omniscient narration",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Dracula",
      "agent": "compilation"
    },
    {
      "text": "This compilation emulates forensic or archival evidence, fostering verisimilitude by framing implausible supernatural occurrences as aggregated firsthand testimonies subject to cross-verification, rather than omniscient narration",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Dracula",
      "agent": "compilation"
    },
    {
      "text": "His recovery from ensuing brain fever underscores physical endurance, though lingering trauma manifests in sharpened senses detecting Dracula's proximity",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Dracula",
