      "agent": "propositions"
    },
    {
      "text": "Propositions in arguments carry truth values\u2014true or false\u2014that influence the overall assessment of the inference's strength",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "propositions"
    },
    {
      "text": "Monotonicity refers to the property that adding further premises to a valid argument cannot invalidate the conclusion; the entailment remains intact or strengthens",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "property"
    },
    {
      "text": "Monotonicity refers to the property that adding further premises to a valid argument cannot invalidate the conclusion; the entailment remains intact or strengthens",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "property"
    },
    {
      "text": "Monotonicity refers to the property that adding further premises to a valid argument cannot invalidate the conclusion; the entailment remains intact or strengthens",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "property"
    },
    {
      "text": "Deductive reasoning forms the foundation for proofs in formal logical systems, where arguments are constructed and verified to establish entailments rigorously",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "deductive"
    },
    {
      "text": "Deductive reasoning forms the foundation for proofs in formal logical systems, where arguments are constructed and verified to establish entailments rigorously",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "deductive"
    },
    {
      "text": "Deductive reasoning forms the foundation for proofs in formal logical systems, where arguments are constructed and verified to establish entailments rigorously",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "deductive"
    },
    {
      "text": "In contrast to ampliative reasoning, which allows for conclusions that go beyond the premises with some uncertainty, deductive methods provide conclusive certainty when premises hold",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "ampliative"
    },
    {
      "text": "In contrast to ampliative reasoning, which allows for conclusions that go beyond the premises with some uncertainty, deductive methods provide conclusive certainty when premises hold",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "ampliative"
    },
    {
      "text": "In contrast to ampliative reasoning, which allows for conclusions that go beyond the premises with some uncertainty, deductive methods provide conclusive certainty when premises hold",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "ampliative"
    },
    {
      "text": "In scientific contexts, abductive steps have driven discoveries, such as hypothesizing Neptune's existence to explain irregularities in Uranus's orbit",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "driven"
    },
    {
      "text": "In scientific contexts, abductive steps have driven discoveries, such as hypothesizing Neptune's existence to explain irregularities in Uranus's orbit",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "driven"
    },
    {
      "text": "In scientific contexts, abductive steps have driven discoveries, such as hypothesizing Neptune's existence to explain irregularities in Uranus's orbit",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "driven"
    },
    {
      "text": "Formal fallacies involve invalid logical structures that fail to preserve truth from premises to conclusion, detectable through analysis of the argument's form",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "fallacies"
    },
    {
      "text": "Formal fallacies involve invalid logical structures that fail to preserve truth from premises to conclusion, detectable through analysis of the argument's form",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "fallacies"
    },
    {
      "text": "Formal fallacies involve invalid logical structures that fail to preserve truth from premises to conclusion, detectable through analysis of the argument's form",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "fallacies"
    },
    {
      "text": "Compound propositions are constructed by applying connectives to atomic or other compound propositions",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "propositions"
    },
    {
      "text": "Compound propositions are constructed by applying connectives to atomic or other compound propositions",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
