    },
    {
      "text": "In total, 38 plays are attributed to him, many premiered by his company at these venues",
      "source": "Grokipedia-William_Shakespeare",
      "agent": "plays"
    },
    {
      "text": "Performed likely around 1594, it reflects the period's social norms around courtship while showcasing Shakespeare's inventive use of induction frames and subplots",
      "source": "Grokipedia-William_Shakespeare",
      "agent": "while"
    },
    {
      "text": "Scholarly analysis confirms that Shakespeare's adherence to five acts was conventional among educated Elizabethans, yet he often blurred act divisions in performance to maintain fluidity",
      "source": "Grokipedia-William_Shakespeare",
      "agent": "among"
    },
    {
      "text": "In the 20th century, Shakespeare's reputation underwent rigorous academic scrutiny, beginning with the formalist approaches of New Criticism exemplified by A",
      "source": "Grokipedia-William_Shakespeare",
      "agent": "century"
    },
    {
      "text": "UNESCO recognizes Shakespeare's archival documents as part of its Memory of the World Programme , underscoring their enduring value in global cultural heritage and education",
      "source": "Grokipedia-William_Shakespeare",
      "agent": "unesco"
    },
    {
      "text": "Shakespeare's legacy manifests in global holidays and monuments that commemorate his life and contributions",
      "source": "Grokipedia-William_Shakespeare",
      "agent": "shakespeare"
    },
    {
      "text": "Politically, Shakespeare navigated Elizabethan and Jacobean sensitivities with apparent neutrality, avoiding overt challenges that could invite censorship while subtly mirroring contemporary issues",
      "source": "Grokipedia-William_Shakespeare",
      "agent": "shakespeare"
    },
    {
      "text": "No biographical evidence confirms these as personal confessions, and interpretations remain speculative",
      "source": "Grokipedia-William_Shakespeare",
      "agent": "biographical"
    },
    {
      "text": "Bennet have five unmarried daughters\u2014Jane, Elizabeth, Mary, Catherine (Kitty), and Lydia\u2014whose futures depend on advantageous marriages due to the estate's entailment to a male cousin",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "bennet"
    },
    {
      "text": "Collins's proposal despite the entailment of the Longbourn estate threatening her family's future",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "collins"
    },
    {
      "text": "Bennet's vulgarity and the family's lack of connections",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "bennet"
    },
    {
      "text": "Bennet , preoccupied with securing husbands for her daughters to avoid destitution post-Mr",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "bennet"
    },
    {
      "text": "Collins's proposal despite financial pressures, prioritizing integrity over immediate security; this counters sentimental excess, as seen in Lydia's ruinous impulsivity",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "collins"
    },
    {
      "text": "Bennet's frantic efforts to match her daughters with eligible men like Mr",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "bennet"
    },
    {
      "text": "Bingley , whose \u00a35,000 yearly income represents stability absent in their own precarious future",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "bingley"
    },
    {
      "text": "Bennet's vulgar matchmaking and the younger sisters' unrestrained flirtations at balls",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "bennet"
    },
    {
      "text": "Bennet's sardonic detachment from his parental role\u2014favoring personal amusement over daughters' moral or social preparation\u2014exacerbates the entailment's threat, while Mrs",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "bennet"
    },
    {
      "text": "Collins, whose sycophancy toward Lady Catherine de Bourgh mocks clerical patronage and aristocratic pretensions",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "collins"
    },
    {
      "text": "Collins, satirizing primogeniture's economic irrationality for female dependents",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "collins"
    },
    {
      "text": "In November 1797, her father George Austen submitted the manuscript to London publisher Thomas Cadell, who declined to publish it without comment",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "austen"
    },
    {
      "text": "Austen had sold the copyright to Egerton for \u00a3110, though she had sought \u00a3150 for it",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "austen"
    },
    {
      "text": "In America , recognition lagged during the century's first half, with broader appreciation emerging later as Austen's oeuvre gained transatlantic footing",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "recognition"
    },
    {
      "text": "Leavis's The Great Tradition (1948), positioned Austen within the English novelistic canon but subordinated Pride and Prejudice to Mansfield Park for its greater moral seriousness",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "tradition"
    },
    {
      "text": "Bennet's death, where the estate passes to Mr",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "bennet"
    },
    {
      "text": "Johnson have described the work as Jane Austen's most conservative, an \"imaginative experiment with conservative myths\" that privileges prudence and humility over libertarian impulses",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "johnson"
    },
    {
      "text": "Andrew Davies' stage rendition, derived from his 1995 television script, has been performed in regional theaters, highlighting the Bennet sisters' domestic dynamics and Darcy's transformation",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "rendition"
    },
    {
      "text": "Collins and Lady Catherine de Bourgh",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Pride_and_Prejudice",
      "agent": "collins"
    },
    {
      "text": "Revised editions in 1823 and 1831 bore Shelley's name and incorporated alterations reflecting her maturing views on determinism and fate",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Frankenstein",
      "agent": "name"
    },
    {
      "text": "Ultimately, the work critiques unchecked innatism by portraying social neglect as the decisive catalyst for monstrosity, urging responsibility in creators and communities",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Frankenstein",
      "agent": "critiques"
    },
    {
      "text": "He shares lodgings at 221B Baker Street with his friend and narrator, Dr",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "shares"
    },
    {
      "text": "John Watson, a former army surgeon whose accounts form the basis of the stories",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "john"
    },
    {
      "text": "Doyle drew inspiration for Holmes's deductive prowess from his medical professor Dr",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "doyle"
    },
    {
      "text": "Joseph Bell , whose emphasis on diagnosing ailments through careful observation of physical clues influenced the character's crime-solving methodology",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "emphasis"
    },
    {
      "text": "His father, Charles Altamont Doyle, worked as a civil servant and artist but struggled with alcoholism and mental illness, leading to institutionalization by 1881",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "doyle"
    },
    {
      "text": "Doyle began medical studies at the University of Edinburgh in 1876, graduating with an MB and CM in 1881",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "medical"
    },
    {
      "text": "Influences from Real-Life Figures and Events Dr",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "influences"
    },
    {
      "text": "Joseph Bell , a surgeon and lecturer at the University of Edinburgh's Royal Infirmary, served as the primary real-life model for Sherlock Holmes's deductive methodology",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "infirmary"
    },
    {
      "text": "Bell's technique relied on empirical observation and logical elimination rather than guesswork, mirroring Holmes's emphasis on evidence-based reasoning over intuition",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "technique"
    },
    {
      "text": "While Bell lacked Holmes's fictional infallibility, his real-world consultations with Edinburgh police on criminal cases prefigured the consulting detective archetype",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "while"
    },
    {
      "text": "Real-life events indirectly influenced Holmes's world-building, drawing from Victorian London's criminal landscape and emerging police science",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "events"
    },
    {
      "text": "The narrative introduced Holmes as a \"consulting detective\" employing deductive reasoning and forensic observation, alongside his narrator and companion, Dr",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "narrative"
    },
    {
      "text": "Watson, a recently invalided army surgeon",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "watson"
    },
    {
      "text": "Public backlash was intense, with widespread mourning\u2014readers wore black armbands, over 20,000 canceled Strand subscriptions, and pleas flooded Doyle, including from Queen Victoria",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "public"
    },
    {
      "text": "Across Doyle's canon\u2014four novels and 56 short stories\u2014Holmes' core traits remained consistent: superhuman observation, disdain for the mundane, and reliance on empirical evidence over intuition",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "holmes"
    },
    {
      "text": "The character's enduring appeal stemmed from this archetype's rigidity, enabling readers to engage with puzzle-solving vicariously through Watson's perspective",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "s"
    },
    {
      "text": "Doyle ceased new Holmes stories after 1927, completing four further collections post-resurrection",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "doyle"
    },
    {
      "text": "His maternal grandmother was the sister of the French painter Horace Vernet, linking the family to artistic lineage",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "vernet"
    },
    {
      "text": "He attended an unnamed English university , where he focused on scientific studies, particularly chemistry , and conducted independent experiments",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "he"
    },
    {
      "text": "He left university without pursuing a conventional degree , instead honing skills that later defined his consulting detective practice",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "he"
    },
    {
      "text": "Despite this, he rejoins Holmes for critical investigations, including the pursuit of Moriarty culminating in 1891",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "holmes"
    },
    {
      "text": "These cases, spanning thefts, murders, and conspiracies from 1881 to 1903, showcased Holmes's versatility against adversaries ranging from opportunistic criminals to organized syndicates",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "these"
    },
    {
      "text": "The pursuit leads Holmes and Dr",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "pursuit"
    },
    {
      "text": "Watson to the Continent, culminating at the Reichenbach Falls near Meiringen , Switzerland , on 4 May 1891",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "watson"
    },
    {
      "text": "Holmes sends Watson ahead to an inn on a fabricated pretext of illness, allowing him to confront Moriarty alone at the falls' edge",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "watson"
    },
    {
      "text": "A violent struggle ensues, after which both men plummet into the chasm below and are presumed drowned",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "violent"
    },
    {
      "text": "Watson resumes medical practice, marries, and publishes an account of Holmes's exploits, inadvertently alerting Moriarty's surviving lieutenant, Colonel Sebastian Moran, to potential threats",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "watson"
    },
    {
      "text": "Holmes, however, survives the fall by employing baritsu\u2014a form of Japanese wrestling\u2014to hurl Moriarty to his death while gripping a rocky outcrop",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "holmes"
    },
    {
      "text": "During the Hiatus , Holmes undertakes extensive travels to elude recognition and pursue personal inquiries",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "during"
    },
    {
      "text": "He journeys to Tibet , residing in Lhasa under the alias \"Sig",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "he"
    },
    {
      "text": "Holmes returns to London in April 1894 upon intelligence of Moran 's plot against him, disguised as an elderly bookseller",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "holmes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Holmes's Sussex phase features prominently in three late stories, underscoring that retirement does not preclude occasional deduction",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "holmes"
    },
    {
      "text": "These cases illustrate Holmes applying his faculties locally, without Watson's presence, emphasizing self-reliant reasoning in a rural setting",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "these"
    },
    {
      "text": "Doyle's portrayal thus balances Holmes's cerebral pursuits with residual vigilance, rooted in the character's aversion to idleness amid solvable enigmas",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "holmes"
    },
    {
      "text": "This practice, observed by Watson in The Sign of Four (1890), underscores Holmes's self-reliant approach to maintaining mental acuity absent intellectual stimulation",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "practice"
    },
    {
      "text": "His bohemianism extends to disregard for personal appearance during investigations, adopting disguises from beggar to clergyman with ease, prioritizing efficacy over propriety",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "appearance"
    },
    {
      "text": "Holmes employs a variety of revolver s, including short-barreled Webley models suitable for concealed carry and rapid deployment",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "holmes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Financially supported by lucrative client fees, Holmes sustains these without fixed income, funding irregular assistants and occasional travel",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "financially"
    },
    {
      "text": "Relationships and Social Views Bond with Watson Dr",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "relationships"
    },
    {
      "text": "Their interactions, though infrequent, reveal a pragmatic alliance rooted in mutual respect for intellect rather than affection",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "alliance"
    },
    {
      "text": "Similarly, during the Moriarty confrontation in \"The Final Problem\" (1893), Mycroft handles financial settlements for Sherlock's presumed demise, demonstrating understated fraternal support amid crisis",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "similarly"
    },
    {
      "text": "Her portrayal challenges Victorian femininity norms, blending allure with strategic acumen, though Holmes's respect remains intellectual, not amorous",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "victorian"
    },
    {
      "text": "Holmes's interactions affirm capability where present, fostering narrative tension between his skepticism and empirical encounters with women's resourcefulness",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "holmes"
    },
    {
      "text": "These works established Holmes's character, deductive methods, and partnership with Dr",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "works"
    },
    {
      "text": "John Watson, often blending intricate mysteries with historical or exotic backdrops",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "watson"
    },
    {
      "text": "Unlike the short stories serialized in magazines, the novels allowed for more expansive narratives, including detailed backstories and multi-part structures",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "unlike"
    },
    {
      "text": "Doyle received \u00a325 for the rights, reflecting its initial modest reception before later reprints",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "doyle"
    },
    {
      "text": "The title refers to a secret society's mark on a map",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "title"
    },
    {
      "text": "The novel sold 50,000 copies in weeks, boosting Doyle's popularity",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "novel"
    },
    {
      "text": "Doran in February 1915, draws from the Mollie Maguires and Professor Moriarty's shadow",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "doran"
    },
    {
      "text": "These volumes established Holmes's deductive methods and partnership with Dr",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "volumes"
    },
    {
      "text": "John Watson through episodic cases involving crimes, mysteries, and intrigue set mostly in Victorian and Edwardian London",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "watson"
    },
    {
      "text": "This led to a hiatus in new Holmes tales until public demand prompted revival",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "holmes"
    },
    {
      "text": "These collections, totaling 56 stories, form the core of the Holmes canon alongside the four novels",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "collections"
    },
    {
      "text": "However, some contemporaries dismissed the genre as sensationalist, prioritizing Doyle's historical novels like The White Company (1891) as superior artistic endeavors",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "however"
    },
    {
      "text": "Doyle himself harbored growing ambivalence toward the series, viewing Holmes as a distraction from his preferred pursuits in historical fiction and spiritualist writings",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "doyle"
    },
    {
      "text": "This tension persisted, as Doyle prioritized other genres even after knighthood in 1902, partly tied to his Boer War advocacy rather than Holmes's fame",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "tension"
    },
    {
      "text": "Similar organizations, such as the Sherlock Holmes Society of London , host events and publications fostering analysis of the canon",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "holmes"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Sherlock Holmes Museum at 221B Baker Street in London recreates the fictional residence, drawing international visitors to experience preserved artifacts evoking the stories' setting",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "sherlock"
    },
    {
      "text": "This approach marked a shift from earlier detective tales, such as those by Edgar Allan Poe featuring C",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "approach"
    },
    {
      "text": "Auguste Dupin , by emphasizing methodical reasoning and forensic techniques, thereby elevating the genre's intellectual rigor and realism",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "dupin"
    },
    {
      "text": "Holmes's narratives popularized key conventions, including the first-person narration by a reliable companion ( Dr",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "holmes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Watson ), which provided readers with partial clues while concealing the detective's full deductions until the revelation, heightening suspense and reader engagement",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "watson"
    },
    {
      "text": "Similarly, cigar ash classification, with Holmes distinguishing over 140 varieties to link suspects to scenes, highlighted micro-trace evidence potential, predating formalized trace analysis",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "similarly"
    },
    {
      "text": "These narratives promoted crime scene preservation, with Holmes decrying contamination by officials, a practice echoed in modern protocols prioritizing evidence integrity and spatial relationships",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "narratives"
    },
    {
      "text": "Critics note that Holmes' infallible deductions idealized processes, yet the emphasis on science over intuition shifted investigative paradigms toward data-driven analysis",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "critics"
    },
    {
      "text": "Pastiches, which imitate Doyle's narrative voice and structure while using Holmes and Watson directly, proliferated in the 20th century",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "while"
    },
    {
      "text": "Another influential anthology, The Misadventures of Sherlock Holmes edited by Ellery Queen (Frederic Dannay and Manfred B",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "holmes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Henry and Agatha Christie, blending homages with lighter imitations to showcase the character's versatility",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "christie"
    },
    {
      "text": "Parodies, often employing exaggeration or altered names to satirize Holmes's deductive prowess and Victorian tropes, emerged soon after the character's 1887 debut",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "parodies"
    },
    {
      "text": "This preceded Arthur Conan Doyle's publication of \" The Final Problem \" and omitted Professor Moriarty",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "this"
    },
    {
      "text": "These audio formats emphasized Holmes's deductive monologues and atmospheric sound design , sustaining the character's popularity through voice-only storytelling",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "formats"
    },
    {
      "text": "Its sequel, Sherlock Holmes: A Game of Shadows (2011), escalated the action against Professor Moriarty, portrayed by Jared Harris, and grossed $545 million worldwide",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "holmes"
    },
    {
      "text": "These entries emphasized Holmes's physical prowess and deductive methods through slow-motion fight reconstructions, diverging from Doyle's narrative focus on cerebral investigation",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "entries"
    },
    {
      "text": "7 million UK viewers for its premiere",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "million"
    },
    {
      "text": "Joan Watson, a former surgeon turned sober companion , ran for seven seasons and 154 episodes, modernizing cases with contemporary forensics while retaining Holmes's eccentricities",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "watson"
    },
    {
      "text": "By 2025, projects like Sherlock Holmes 3 , continuing Ritchie's franchise with Downey Jr",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "holmes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Watson expresses concern over the practice's potential for harm",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "watson"
    },
    {
      "text": "Holmes also references occasional use of morphine, a one-percent solution, for similar purposes of alleviating boredom, though such instances are less frequent in the canon",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "holmes"
    },
    {
      "text": "This framework avoids outright condemnation, framing Holmes' ambiguities as products of his era's tensions between individual agency and institutional authority, without endorsing them as unproblematic",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "framework"
    },
    {
      "text": "Such representations echo empirical data from the era, including higher conviction rates among laborers documented in Victorian criminal statistics",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "era"
    },
    {
      "text": "This mirrors legal realities, such as pre-1882 property laws subordinating married women's assets to husbands, and limited female suffrage until partial enfranchisement in 1918",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "laws"
    },
    {
      "text": "Watson's backstory, wounded at the Battle of Maiwand in 1880 during the Second Anglo-Afghan War, underscores military exploits sustaining empire",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "watson"
    },
    {
      "text": "Holmes neutralizes these \"exotic\" perils, symbolizing Britain's self-conceived role in containing imperial blowback",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "holmes"
    },
    {
      "text": "Similarly, CBS's Elementary (2012\u20132019) transposes stories to New York City , with Lucy Liu as a female Dr",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "elementary"
    },
    {
      "text": "Joan Watson assisting Jonny Lee Miller's recovering addict Holmes",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "watson"
    },
    {
      "text": "These approaches, while enriching discussion, necessitate scrutiny to distinguish interpretive license from distortion, privileging primary texts over secondary impositions",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Sherlock_Holmes",
      "agent": "while"
    },
    {
      "text": "In its strongest forms, epistemic absolutism holds that propositional knowledge is an ungradable, binary concept: one either knows or does not know, without degrees",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Epistemology",
      "agent": "either"
    },
    {
      "text": "In its strongest forms, epistemic absolutism holds that propositional knowledge is an ungradable, binary concept: one either knows or does not know, without degrees",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Epistemology",
      "agent": "either"
    },
    {
      "text": "Kant's first formulation requires acting only on maxims that can be willed as universal laws without contradiction, ensuring actions stem from duty rather than inclination",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Ethics",
      "agent": "kant"
    },
    {
      "text": "Similar findings extend to 3-month-olds, who show differential responses to helpful versus hindering characters, implying that rudimentary moral intuitions emerge prior to significant cultural exposure",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Ethics",
      "agent": "similar"
    },
    {
      "text": "Society of New York Hospital (1914), affirming patients' right to bodily integrity , and solidified post-Nuremberg Code (1947) after unethical experiments exposed coercion risks",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Ethics",
      "agent": "society"
    },
    {
      "text": "Ethical knowledge derives from contemplating eternal Forms, particularly the Form of the Good , enabling rulers\u2014philosopher-kings\u2014to govern justly",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Ethics",
      "agent": "derives"
    },
    {
      "text": "Stoicism , founded by Zeno of Citium (c",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Ethics",
      "agent": "stoicism"
    },
    {
      "text": "Continental philosophy diverged with existentialism , as Jean-Paul Sartre's Being and Nothingness (1943) asserted radical human freedom entails self-created values amid absurdity, rejecting deterministic ethics",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Ethics",
      "agent": "philosophy"
    },
    {
      "text": "Anscombe's 1958 essay \"Modern Moral Philosophy,\" which lambasted obligation-based ethics divorced from virtues or divine law , urging a return to Aristotelian character-centered approaches",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Ethics",
      "agent": "philosophy"
    },
    {
      "text": "Belnap in the 1950s\u20131970s, the logic rejects classical implications that permit irrelevant premises, instead demanding shared variables or content between antecedent and consequent in implications",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Logic",
      "agent": "logic"
    },
