    {
      "text": "Building on this, Newell, Simon, and J",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Artificial_intelligence",
      "agent": "building"
    },
    {
      "text": "In professional services, AI's concentration of gains in cognitive tasks supports higher output per worker, though realization depends on complementary human skills and infrastructure investments",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Artificial_intelligence",
      "agent": "professional"
    },
    {
      "text": "AI's causal limitations \u2014such as inability to model dynamic biological interactions fully\u2014constrain its standalone reliability in complex treatment contexts",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Artificial_intelligence",
      "agent": "causal"
    },
    {
      "text": "Empirical firm surveys confirm about 27% of AI implementations replace specific tasks, but broader adoption enhances complementary human skills in non-routine domains",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Artificial_intelligence",
      "agent": "empirical"
    },
    {
      "text": "Adaptability Narrow; requires retraining for novelty",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Artificial_intelligence",
      "agent": "narrow"
    },
    {
      "text": "Newman projections visualize conformational isomers by projecting along a C-C bond, showing front and back substituents as circles, introduced by Melvin S",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Organic_chemistry",
      "agent": "newman"
    },
    {
      "text": "Seebach's dithiane methodology extends this by masking carbonyls as umpolung reagents, alkylating at the carbon, and unmasking to regenerate the carbonyl",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Organic_chemistry",
      "agent": "s"
    },
    {
      "text": "Miller in 1976 and refined by Michael O",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Number_theory",
      "agent": "miller"
    },
    {
      "text": "Linear Data Structures Arrays are contiguous blocks of memory that store elements of the same type, allowing constant-time access to any element via indexing",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Computer_science",
      "agent": "linear"
    },
    {
      "text": "Collisions, where multiple keys hash to the same index, are resolved through chaining (using linked lists per slot) or open addressing (probing alternative slots)",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Computer_science",
      "agent": "index"
    },
    {
      "text": "Cognitive aspects of HCI model human performance in interaction tasks, exemplified by Fitts' Law, which quantifies pointing time in graphical interfaces",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Computer_science",
      "agent": "model"
    },
    {
      "text": "Data immutability means structures cannot be altered after creation; instead, new versions are produced, which supports safe sharing and reduces errors from unintended modifications",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Computer_science",
      "agent": "means"
    },
    {
      "text": "Sexual Selection and Recombination Sexual selection constitutes a form of natural selection wherein traits evolve primarily through differential success in mating rather than survival advantages",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Evolution",
      "agent": "success"
    },
    {
      "text": "Mainstream adoption of EES remains contested, with some reviews attributing resistance to entrenched gene-centrism in academia rather than evidential deficits",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Evolution",
      "agent": "remains"
    },
    {
      "text": "Vaccines resist evolutionary escape better than drugs because they target conserved epitopes and elicit broad immune responses, reducing mutation incentives compared to monotherapy pressures",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Evolution",
      "agent": "target"
    },
    {
      "text": "Cleavage refers to the tendency to break along flat planes parallel to weak atomic bonds, as seen in mica's perfect basal cleavage",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Geology",
      "agent": "bonds"
    },
    {
      "text": "Rocks Rocks are naturally occurring solid aggregates of one or more minerals or mineraloids, forming the foundational materials of Earth's crust",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Geology",
      "agent": "naturally"
    },
    {
      "text": "Sedimentary rocks originate from the accumulation and lithification of sediments derived from weathering , erosion , or precipitation",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Geology",
      "agent": "sedimentary"
    },
    {
      "text": "Volcanism and Magmatism Volcanism and magmatism involve the generation, ascent, and eruption of magma from Earth's interior, primarily driven by tectonic processes",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Geology",
      "agent": "volcanism"
    },
    {
      "text": "Basaltic magma, low in silica (45-55 wt%), is fluid due to low viscosity, enabling effusive eruptions and forming mafic rocks like basalt",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Geology",
      "agent": "magma"
    },
    {
      "text": "Shield volcanoes, built from fluid basaltic lava flows, form broad, gently sloping domes like those in Hawaii",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Geology",
      "agent": "form"
    },
    {
      "text": "Monitoring relies on seismicity to detect magma movement\u2014such as long-period earthquakes signaling fluid migration\u2014and gas emissions, where elevated sulfur dioxide indicates rising magma",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Geology",
      "agent": "period"
    },
    {
      "text": "Globally, approximately 500 volcanoes are active, with about 50-70 erupting annually, concentrated along the Pacific Ring of Fire where subduction drives intense magmatism",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Geology",
      "agent": "50"
    },
    {
      "text": "Unconformities represent gaps in the geological record where erosion or non-deposition has removed strata, indicating periods of missing time between older and younger rock units",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Geology",
      "agent": "geological"
    },
    {
      "text": "For example, elevated levels of incompatible elements such as zirconium can signal fractional crystallization in basalts",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Geology",
      "agent": "example"
    },
    {
      "text": "In contrast, brittle deformation dominates near the surface or under rapid loading, resulting in fractures and faults",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Geology",
      "agent": "deformation"
    },
    {
      "text": "Coal reserves exceed 1 trillion tons, concentrated in the U",
