    },
    {
      "text": "Sources aligned with free-market perspectives highlight how such rules export precautionary overreach extraterritorially, affecting U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Internet",
      "agent": "sources"
    },
    {
      "text": "Regulatory and infrastructural hurdles further impede access, including spectrum allocation delays and bureaucratic permitting that slow network rollout in underserved regions",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Internet",
      "agent": "access"
    },
    {
      "text": "Efforts like satellite initiatives show promise for geographic isolation but remain constrained by latency and cost scalability",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Internet",
      "agent": "initiatives"
    },
    {
      "text": "Integration with 5G / 6G via non-terrestrial network standards promises seamless handoffs, enhancing internet resilience against terrestrial outages",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Internet",
      "agent": "integration"
    },
    {
      "text": "Adoption metrics as of 2025 show growth but limited mainstream penetration: the global crypto adoption index ranks India and the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Internet",
      "agent": "adoption"
    },
    {
      "text": "Adoption metrics as of 2025 show growth but limited mainstream penetration: the global crypto adoption index ranks India and the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Internet",
      "agent": "adoption"
    },
    {
      "text": "Adoption metrics as of 2025 show growth but limited mainstream penetration: the global crypto adoption index ranks India and the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Internet",
      "agent": "adoption"
    },
    {
      "text": "Adoption metrics as of 2025 show growth but limited mainstream penetration: the global crypto adoption index ranks India and the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Internet",
      "agent": "adoption"
    },
    {
      "text": "Adoption metrics as of 2025 show growth but limited mainstream penetration: the global crypto adoption index ranks India and the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Internet",
      "agent": "adoption"
    },
    {
      "text": "Adoption metrics as of 2025 show growth but limited mainstream penetration: the global crypto adoption index ranks India and the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Internet",
      "agent": "adoption"
    },
    {
      "text": "Scalability constraints limit throughput\u2014 Ethereum processes around 15-30 transactions per second versus Visa's 1,700\u2014leading to high fees during congestion, which discourages mass adoption",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Internet",
      "agent": "limit"
    },
    {
      "text": "Empirical data suggests that while blockchain reduces certain intermediaries, new forms of centralization emerge via influential developers or validators, challenging the narrative of pure decentralization",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Internet",
      "agent": "empirical"
    },
    {
      "text": "This integration reduces human intervention in tasks such as configuration testing and deployment, addressing the complexity of scaling networks for data-intensive applications",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Internet",
      "agent": "configuration"
    },
    {
      "text": "Essential to Internet functionality, DNS supports not only address lookup but also other resource records for mail exchange, security certificates, and service discovery",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "dns"
    },
    {
      "text": "Initial implementations deployed in 1984 facilitated the transition to a delegated, tree-structured hierarchy rooted at thirteen top-level servers, promoting fault tolerance and global distribution",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "implementations"
    },
    {
      "text": "Ongoing security concerns highlight the tension between DNS's original simplicity for performance and the causal risks of unverified data propagation in a trust-minimized environment",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "dns"
    },
    {
      "text": "Resolution processes embody key operational principles, employing either recursive or iterative queries over UDP or TCP on port 53",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "resolution"
    },
    {
      "text": "In recursive resolution, a name server assumes responsibility for fully resolving the query by querying other servers if necessary, though this is optional and resource-intensive",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "name"
    },
    {
      "text": "The protocol's message format, including header flags for recursion desired (RD) and authoritative answers (AA), further supports efficient, fault-tolerant operation without central points of failure",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "format"
    },
    {
      "text": "These operational constraints prompted the design of a distributed naming system",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "constraints"
    },
    {
      "text": "As internet globalization accelerated, apprehensions about unilateral U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "globalization"
    },
    {
      "text": "As internet globalization accelerated, apprehensions about unilateral U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "globalization"
    },
    {
      "text": "These developments solidified DNS as the de facto global naming standard, underpinning the internet 's transition from academic tool to commercial ecosystem",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "developments"
    },
    {
      "text": "Evolution and Key Milestones (2000s-Present) The 2000s marked a shift toward securing and expanding the DNS amid growing internet scale and vulnerabilities",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "2000s"
    },
    {
      "text": "Internationalization efforts advanced concurrently, enabling non-ASCII domain names",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "efforts"
    },
    {
      "text": "This was updated in 2008\u20132010 via RFC 5890\u20135894 (IDNA2008), refining string preparation and bidirectional text handling to better support global scripts",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "this"
    },
    {
      "text": "The 2010s saw DNS architecture scale with TLD proliferation and privacy enhancements",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "2010s"
    },
    {
      "text": "Recent developments emphasize resilience and encryption ubiquity",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "recent"
    },
    {
      "text": "The September 2016 completion of the IANA stewardship transition transferred oversight of root zone changes from U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "transition"
    },
    {
      "text": "The September 2016 completion of the IANA stewardship transition transferred oversight of root zone changes from U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "transition"
    },
    {
      "text": "Encrypted DNS protocols faced scrutiny for potential censorship enablement, prompting standards like Oblivious DoH (RFC 9230, 2022) to anonymize queries further",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "dns"
    },
    {
      "text": "ICANN 's next gTLD application round is slated for 2026, promising additional expansions amid debates on namespace saturation",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "icann"
    },
    {
      "text": "These milestones reflect DNS's adaptation to a trillion-query-per-day scale , prioritizing integrity against evolving threats like DDoS amplification and state-sponsored spoofing",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "dns"
    },
    {
      "text": "The total length of a domain name, including label lengths and content, must not exceed 255 octets",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "name"
    },
    {
      "text": "Comparisons of domain names are case-insensitive, though original case is preserved in storage and transmission where feasible",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "case"
    },
    {
      "text": "Sibling nodes under the same parent must have unique labels, ensuring unambiguous resolution within the hierarchy",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "labels"
    },
    {
      "text": "The structure's tree-like nature, akin to file systems, enables efficient traversal during resolution, starting from the root and descending through delegated authorities",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "structure"
    },
    {
      "text": "Domain Name Syntax and Internationalization Domain names consist of a sequence of labels separated by dots, forming a hierarchical structure that maps to network resources",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "syntax"
    },
    {
      "text": "Each label represents a node in the DNS namespace and must adhere to strict syntactic rules defined in RFC 1035",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "dns"
    },
    {
      "text": "Domain names are case-insensitive, meaning \"example",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "case"
    },
    {
      "text": "Domain names are case-insensitive, meaning \"example",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "case"
    },
    {
      "text": "com\" resolves equivalently to \"EXAMPLE",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "resolves"
    },
    {
      "text": "com\" resolves equivalently to \"EXAMPLE",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "resolves"
    },
    {
      "text": "COM\", though conventions favor lowercase representation in documentation and queries",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "though"
    },
    {
      "text": "COM\", though conventions favor lowercase representation in documentation and queries",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "though"
    },
    {
      "text": "Punycode ensures lossless round-trip conversion between Unicode and ASCII forms, handling up to the 63-octet label limit while preserving the total domain length restriction",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "punycode"
    },
    {
      "text": "Deployment began with top-level domain support, such as",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "deployment"
    },
    {
      "text": "Deployment began with top-level domain support, such as",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "deployment"
    },
    {
      "text": "\u0440\u0444 for Russian in 2010, enabling native-script registrations while maintaining backward compatibility with ASCII-only resolvers",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "\u0440\u0444"
    },
    {
      "text": "\u0440\u0444 for Russian in 2010, enabling native-script registrations while maintaining backward compatibility with ASCII-only resolvers",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "\u0440\u0444"
    },
    {
      "text": "Applications must implement ToASCII and ToUnicode operations to handle IDN display and resolution correctly",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "applications"
    },
    {
      "text": "Authoritative name server s maintain complete, up-to-date records for their assigned zone s, prioritizing this data over any cached information during query processing",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "name"
    },
    {
      "text": "This redundancy ensures fault tolerance , as DNS mandates at least two name server s per zone to mitigate single points of failure",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "redundancy"
    },
    {
      "text": "Parent zones do not store data for delegated subzones, instead referring queries to the listed name servers, thereby enforcing the hierarchical partitioning of administrative control",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "store"
    },
    {
      "text": "Army Research Laboratory controls H; Netnod runs I; RIPE NCC manages K; ICANN operates L; and the WIDE Project handles M",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "icann"
    },
    {
      "text": "This occurs through a hierarchical query mechanism involving multiple levels of name servers, starting from the client's local resolver and progressing to authoritative sources",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "mechanism"
    },
    {
      "text": "Root responses include referrals rather than final answers, directing to TLD servers like those for",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "responses"
    },
    {
      "text": "Root responses include referrals rather than final answers, directing to TLD servers like those for",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "responses"
    },
    {
      "text": "com managed by Verisign",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "com"
    },
    {
      "text": "com managed by Verisign",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "com"
    },
    {
      "text": "The full resolution may involve 4-8 round-trip times in uncached scenarios, with average global latency around 30-50 milliseconds due to geographic distribution of servers",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "times"
    },
    {
      "text": "Resolver Types: Recursive, Iterative, and Caching DNS resolvers facilitate the translation of domain names to IP addresses through distinct operational modes: recursive, iterative, and caching",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "dns"
    },
    {
      "text": "Recursive resolution delegates the full query workload to a designated server , while iterative resolution involves step-by-step referrals",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "resolution"
    },
    {
      "text": "Caching enhances both by storing responses to expedite subsequent lookups",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "caching"
    },
    {
      "text": "Recursive resolvers are commonly operated by ISPs or public services like 8",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "commonly"
    },
    {
      "text": "Recursive resolvers are commonly operated by ISPs or public services like 8",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "commonly"
    },
    {
      "text": "8 by Google Public DNS",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "google"
    },
    {
      "text": "8 by Google Public DNS",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "google"
    },
    {
      "text": "The querier then issues a new query to the referred server, repeating the process until the answer is obtained",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "issues"
    },
    {
      "text": "Iterative queries are the standard interaction between resolvers and authoritative name servers, promoting scalability in the global system",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "name"
    },
    {
      "text": "Cache entries include negative responses (NXDOMAIN) to prevent repeated futile lookups",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "entries"
    },
    {
      "text": "Over-reliance on caching can lead to staleness if TTLs are ignored, though protocol enforcement via TTL ensures eventual consistency",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "reliance"
    },
    {
      "text": "Caching-only servers, which neither author data nor recurse beyond cache hits, further optimize local networks by forwarding uncached queries",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "caching"
    },
    {
      "text": "Out-of-domain nameservers, by contrast, do not require glue, as their resolution follows the standard hierarchy without self-reference",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "out"
    },
    {
      "text": "This inversion leverages the hierarchical DNS structure to delegate authority for IP address ranges to network administrators or ISPs",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "this"
    },
    {
      "text": "DNS extends beyond forward and reverse address resolution to support diverse applications via additional resource record types",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "extends"
    },
    {
      "text": "Timeouts trigger retries, typically up to four attempts with exponential backoff starting at 1-2 seconds, to mitigate network variability",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "attempts"
    },
    {
      "text": "The header fields are structured as follows: Field Size (bits) Description ID 16 Unique identifier to associate queries with responses",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "header"
    },
    {
      "text": "AA 1 Authoritative Answer flag, set in responses from authoritative servers",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "authoritative"
    },
    {
      "text": "TC 1 Truncation flag, indicating the message was truncated due to length limits (typically 512 octets for UDP without extensions)",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "tc"
    },
    {
      "text": "RD 1 Recursion Desired, set by the querier to request recursive resolution",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "recursion"
    },
    {
      "text": "RA 1 Recursion Available, set in responses if the server supports recursion",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "recursion"
    },
    {
      "text": "Z 3 Reserved field, must be zero",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "field"
    },
    {
      "text": "QDCOUNT 16 Number of question entries",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "qdcount"
    },
    {
      "text": "ANCOUNT 16 Number of answer resource records",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "ancount"
    },
    {
      "text": "NSCOUNT 16 Number of name server (authority) resource records",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "nscount"
    },
    {
      "text": "ARCOUNT 16 Number of additional resource records",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "arcount"
    },
    {
      "text": "These records are stored in zone files in a textual master format but transmitted in binary wire format during DNS queries and responses",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "records"
    },
    {
      "text": "RR Type Purpose RDATA Format Example A IPv4 address mapping 192",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "example"
    },
    {
      "text": "RR Type Purpose RDATA Format Example A IPv4 address mapping 192",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "example"
    },
    {
      "text": "1 AAAA IPv6 address mapping 2001:db8::1 NS Nameserver designation ns1",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "aaaa"
    },
    {
      "text": "1 AAAA IPv6 address mapping 2001:db8::1 NS Nameserver designation ns1",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "aaaa"
    },
    {
      "text": "CNAME Alias to another name target",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "cname"
    },
    {
      "text": "CNAME Alias to another name target",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "cname"
    },
    {
      "text": "Implementations must adhere to these rules to ensure predictable behavior, as non-compliance has led to interoperability issues in resolvers",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "implementations"
    },
    {
      "text": "This addresses limitations in handling complex responses, such as those required for DNSSEC validation, where signatures and keys increase message size",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "limitations"
    },
    {
      "text": "This encryption prevents passive eavesdropping on query contents, such as domain names, and protects against on-path tampering or injection attacks that exploit unencrypted DNS traffic",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "passive"
    },
    {
      "text": "Following the handshake, DNS queries and responses flow bidirectionally within the TLS stream, preserving the DNS wire format while adding TLS overhead for record-layer protection",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "following"
    },
    {
      "text": "It facilitates network-level management, as the dedicated port allows operators to distinguish and prioritize DoT traffic without deep packet inspection",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "it"
    },
    {
      "text": "Subsequent implementations extended support for features like HTTP/3 over QUIC , though core DoH remains tied to HTTPS /TLS 1",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "implementations"
    },
    {
      "text": "Subsequent implementations extended support for features like HTTP/3 over QUIC , though core DoH remains tied to HTTPS /TLS 1",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "implementations"
    },
    {
      "text": "Adoption accelerated in client software, with Mozilla Firefox enabling DoH by default for U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "adoption"
    },
    {
      "text": "Adoption accelerated in client software, with Mozilla Firefox enabling DoH by default for U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "adoption"
    },
    {
      "text": "However, widespread ISP-level deployment remains limited, as many networks prioritize DNS over TLS (DoT) for centralized control",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "remains"
    },
    {
      "text": "It also resists traffic analysis distinguishing DNS from general HTTPS flows, reducing exposure to man-in-the-middle interference without requiring dedicated DNS ports",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "analysis"
    },
    {
      "text": "Security benefits include opportunistic encryption fallback and certificate pinning to trusted resolvers, though clients must validate server certificates to avoid impersonation",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "benefits"
    },
    {
      "text": "This shift undermines network operators' ability to enforce content filtering for malware blocking or compliance, complicating enterprise security policies and enabling circumvention of lawful intercepts",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "network"
    },
    {
      "text": "Early implementations, such as AdGuard DNS in December 2020, demonstrated practical viability, with benchmarks showing sub-50ms query times under lossy networks",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "dns"
    },
    {
      "text": "As of 2025, it remains one of the most widely deployed encrypted DNS options, with active community maintenance despite competition from standardized alternatives",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "widely"
    },
    {
      "text": "Successful spoofing often relies on predicting query identifiers or exploiting timing to outrace legitimate responses, enabling man-in-the-middle redirection for phishing or malware distribution",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "successful"
    },
    {
      "text": "Globally, around 26% of network service providers and enterprises adopted it by 2023, with validation rates exceeding 30% of queries in regions like the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "providers"
    },
    {
      "text": "Globally, around 26% of network service providers and enterprises adopted it by 2023, with validation rates exceeding 30% of queries in regions like the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "providers"
    },
    {
      "text": "Traffic volumes typically remained below 1 Gbps but focused on server resource exhaustion rather than bandwidth saturation",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "typically"
    },
    {
      "text": "Horrid Hawk, active since February 2023, repurposed hijacked domains for multilingual investment fraud disseminated through Facebook ads",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "2023"
    },
    {
      "text": "DNS tunneling and domain generation algorithms (DGAs) facilitated stealthy data exfiltration and evasion in high-profile compromises",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "dns"
    },
    {
      "text": "DNSSEC implementations revealed operational fragilities, including resource exhaustion vulnerabilities disclosed in early 2024",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "implementations"
    },
    {
      "text": "Configuration errors also caused outages, such as Slack's September 30, 2021, DNSSEC rollout failure\u2014its third attempt\u2014which temporarily broke resolution for slack",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "configuration"
    },
    {
      "text": "Configuration errors also caused outages, such as Slack's September 30, 2021, DNSSEC rollout failure\u2014its third attempt\u2014which temporarily broke resolution for slack",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "configuration"
    },
    {
      "text": "com due to validation mismatches",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "validation"
    },
    {
      "text": "com due to validation mismatches",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "validation"
    },
    {
      "text": "Detection relies on behavioral indicators, such as anomalous query entropy or high-volume TXT record requests, but incomplete logging often permits evasion",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "volume"
    },
    {
      "text": "This confidentiality protects against pervasive surveillance , as outlined in RFC 7258, and reduces risks of data exfiltration through query logging by untrusted parties",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "surveillance"
    },
    {
      "text": "This includes targeting platforms like GitHub and Tor Project , where DNS pollution disrupts access unless mitigated by encrypted DNS protocols",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "this"
    },
    {
      "text": "On March 2, 2020, Roskomnadzor blocked DigitalOcean's DNS servers, affecting hosted independent media",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "roskomnadzor"
    },
    {
      "text": "Geopolitically, domain seizures by Western governments, particularly the United States , represent extraterritorial interference, where U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "geopolitically"
    },
    {
      "text": "Geopolitically, domain seizures by Western governments, particularly the United States , represent extraterritorial interference, where U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "geopolitically"
    },
    {
      "text": "Department of Justice seized 92 domains operated by Iran's Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps on October 7, 2020, for disinformation campaigns, disrupting their resolution worldwide",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "department"
    },
    {
      "text": "Domain Registration Mechanics and Market Dynamics Domain registration occurs through a structured process involving three primary parties: the registrant, the registrar , and the registry",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "mechanics"
    },
    {
      "text": "This structure, inherited from the DNS's origins under U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "structure"
    },
    {
      "text": "This structure, inherited from the DNS's origins under U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "structure"
    },
    {
      "text": "Critics argue this centralization creates vulnerabilities to geopolitical coercion, as evidenced by historical U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "centralization"
    },
    {
      "text": "Critics argue this centralization creates vulnerabilities to geopolitical coercion, as evidenced by historical U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "centralization"
    },
    {
      "text": "Blockchain-based implementations encode domain data as immutable ledger entries, where ownership is proven via private keys rather than revocable registrar contracts",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "blockchain"
    },
    {
      "text": "However, its reliance on proof-of-work mining introduces energy costs, and integration with legacy DNS requires off-chain gateways, limiting seamless adoption",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "however"
    },
    {
      "text": "By July 2023, ENS had registered over 2",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "ens"
    },
    {
      "text": "By July 2023, ENS had registered over 2",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "ens"
    },
    {
      "text": "eth domains, with resolution handled through Ethereum's consensus and off-chain indexers for efficiency",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "eth"
    },
    {
      "text": "eth domains, with resolution handled through Ethereum's consensus and off-chain indexers for efficiency",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "eth"
    },
    {
      "text": "These domains resolve via browser extensions or IPFS gateways, emphasizing user sovereignty over name ownership",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "gateways"
    },
    {
      "text": "Full-scale replacement remains improbable without resolving compatibility and performance gaps, as evidenced by limited resolver support and user inertia favoring established protocols",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "without"
    },
    {
      "text": "Serverless architectures facilitate scalable DNS deployments by abstracting infrastructure management, allowing DNS resolvers and dynamic update services to run on-demand without dedicated servers",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Domain_Name_System",
      "agent": "serverless"
    },
    {
      "text": "The binding of these quarks occurs through the exchange of gluons, the gauge bosons of quantum chromodynamics (QCD), the theory describing the strong nuclear force",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "binding"
    },
    {
      "text": "The proton's wave function in QCD incorporates these elements, describing the probability amplitude for finding quarks of specific flavors and colors in particular configurations",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "s"
    },
    {
      "text": "The proton's mass arises predominantly from this QCD binding energy rather than the bare masses of the quarks",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "mass"
    },
    {
      "text": "Lattice quantum chromodynamics (QCD) simulations offer insights into the proton's internal structure, revealing a pressure profile driven by quark-gluon dynamics",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "structure"
    },
    {
      "text": "This profile, computed from the proton's energy-momentum tensor, underscores the intense confinement within the ~1 fm scale",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "profile"
    },
    {
      "text": "Key searches target supersymmetry-favored modes predicted by grand unified theories",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "key"
    },
    {
      "text": "These results stem from meticulous background modeling, achieving rejection rates below 1 0 \u2212 5 10^{-5} 1 0 \u2212 5 for neutrino-induced events mimicking decays",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "results"
    },
    {
      "text": "An observation of proton decay would confirm grand unified theories by evidencing baryon number non-conservation, unifying the strong, weak, and electromagnetic forces at high energies",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "observation"
    },
    {
      "text": "The proton's stability reflects baryon number conservation in the Standard Model , motivating these null results to constrain physics beyond it",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "model"
    },
    {
      "text": "This conservation is accidental, emerging because no renormalizable terms in the Lagrangian violate baryon number , despite the anomaly in the theory at high energies",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "accidental"
    },
    {
      "text": "Experimental lower limits on the proton lifetime thus impose bounds on m_X, often requiring it to exceed 10^{15} GeV in viable GUTs",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "often"
    },
    {
      "text": "Historical Development Early Discoveries The early investigations into the proton began with J",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "historical"
    },
    {
      "text": "Historical Development Early Discoveries The early investigations into the proton began with J",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "historical"
    },
    {
      "text": "Historical Development Early Discoveries The early investigations into the proton began with J",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "historical"
    },
    {
      "text": "Historical Development Early Discoveries The early investigations into the proton began with J",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "historical"
    },
    {
      "text": "Historical Development Early Discoveries The early investigations into the proton began with J",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "historical"
    },
    {
      "text": "Thomson's studies of cathode rays and positive ions in the late 1890s and early 1900s",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "s"
    },
    {
      "text": "Building on this foundation, Ernest Rutherford's 1911 gold foil experiment marked a pivotal advancement by revealing the atomic nucleus",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "experiment"
    },
    {
      "text": "This discovery implied that the positive charge and most of the mass resided in the nucleus , setting the stage for identifying its fundamental components",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "discovery"
    },
    {
      "text": "Rutherford's subsequent experiments from 1917 to 1919 further characterized the proton through alpha-particle bombardment of light elements",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "further"
    },
    {
      "text": "This nomenclature appeared in his Bakerian Lecture, emphasizing the proton's foundational importance in atomic structure",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "nomenclature"
    },
    {
      "text": "Quark Model and Modern Understanding The quark model emerged in 1964 as a theoretical framework to describe the structure of hadrons, including the proton",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "model"
    },
    {
      "text": "The scaling behavior observed in the cross-sections supported the existence of these quarks as quasi-free partons at short distances",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "scaling"
    },
    {
      "text": "Early lattice calculations focused on hadron masses and matrix elements, yielding insights into the proton's wave function and parton distributions that complement perturbative QCD",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "calculations"
    },
    {
      "text": "This model successfully predicts fission barriers and fusion energetics for heavy elements",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "this"
    },
    {
      "text": "Observations of cosmic microwave background radiation and light element ratios confirm this distribution, providing key evidence for the standard cosmological model",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "light"
    },
    {
      "text": "Beyond stellar fusion, protons dominate primary cosmic rays, making up over 90% of their composition as high-energy particles originating from astrophysical accelerators",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "beyond"
    },
    {
      "text": "This proton flux, detected at Earth after interactions with interstellar medium, provides insights into extreme astrophysical environments and particle acceleration mechanisms",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "flux"
    },
    {
      "text": "These interactions are purely electromagnetic at low energies, with negligible nuclear contributions below several MeV",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "interactions"
    },
    {
      "text": "For low-energy protons (typically below 10 MeV), elastic scattering off atomic nuclei via the Coulomb interaction governs angular deflection, described by the Rutherford scattering formula",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "typically"
    },
    {
      "text": "This formula predicts a strong forward-peaking distribution, with large-angle scatters rare due to the 1 / r 1/r 1/ r Coulomb potential",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "formula"
    },
    {
      "text": "Multiple small-angle scatters accumulate to broaden the proton's trajectory, but single large deflections remain possible",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "multiple"
    },
    {
      "text": "Such detectors operate reliably up to high fluxes, with corrections applied for recombination effects at elevated dose rates",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "operate"
    },
    {
      "text": "As a result, quarks cannot be isolated, and the proton remains a color-neutral hadron",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "result"
    },
    {
      "text": "Such quantum effects underscore the proton's role in facilitating ultrafast proton-coupled electron transfers critical for biological catalysis",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "effects"
    },
    {
      "text": "This frequency range allows for high-resolution spectra that reveal detailed information about the proton's environment in molecules",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "frequency"
    },
    {
      "text": "These shifts are independent of the magnetic field strength, making \u03b4 \\delta \u03b4 a robust measure for identifying functional groups",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "strength"
    },
    {
      "text": "This coupling provides critical insights into proton-proton connectivity and stereochemistry , aiding in the elucidation of molecular frameworks",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "this"
    },
    {
      "text": "Unlike conventional X-ray radiotherapy, where energy deposition decreases exponentially throughout the treatment volume, protons exhibit a characteristic energy deposition profile known as the Bragg peak",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "unlike"
    },
    {
      "text": "The concept of using protons for cancer treatment was first proposed by physicist Robert R",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "concept"
    },
    {
      "text": "Wilson in 1946, recognizing their potential for precise dose delivery",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "1946"
    },
    {
      "text": "For superficial tumors, lower energies suffice, while deeper-seated malignancies require higher energies to position the Bragg peak accurately within the target",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "while"
    },
    {
      "text": "Clinical evidence supports improved outcomes in pediatric cancers and skull base tumors due to this sparing effect",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "supports"
    },
    {
      "text": "For protons, the RBE is approximately 1",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "rbe"
    },
    {
      "text": "For protons, the RBE is approximately 1",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "rbe"
    },
    {
      "text": "For protons, the RBE is approximately 1",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "rbe"
    },
    {
      "text": "For protons, the RBE is approximately 1",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "rbe"
    },
    {
      "text": "For protons, the RBE is approximately 1",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "rbe"
    },
    {
      "text": "1 in biological tissues, meaning proton doses are scaled by this factor to estimate equivalent effects",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "tissues"
    },
    {
      "text": "1 in biological tissues, meaning proton doses are scaled by this factor to estimate equivalent effects",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "tissues"
    },
    {
      "text": "1 in biological tissues, meaning proton doses are scaled by this factor to estimate equivalent effects",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "tissues"
    },
    {
      "text": "1 in biological tissues, meaning proton doses are scaled by this factor to estimate equivalent effects",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "tissues"
    },
    {
      "text": "1 in biological tissues, meaning proton doses are scaled by this factor to estimate equivalent effects",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "tissues"
    },
    {
      "text": "This adjustment helps assess risks from both acute high-dose events and chronic low-level exposures in space environments",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "level"
    },
    {
      "text": "These limits apply broadly to ionizing radiation , including protons, and are enforced to minimize long-term health risks",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "broadly"
    },
    {
      "text": "It carries an electric charge of \u22121 e, a rest mass of 938",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
      "agent": "carries"
    },
    {
      "text": "It carries an electric charge of \u22121 e, a rest mass of 938",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Proton",
