    },
    {
      "text": "1 degrees with bandwidths up to 10 Hz under tuned PID",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "hz"
    },
    {
      "text": "Motion planning , a foundational subset, computes collision-free paths from initial to goal states, with variants incorporating time, uncertainty, or multi-robot coordination",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "motion"
    },
    {
      "text": "Probabilistic Roadmap (PRM) methods, introduced by Kavraki et al",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "methods"
    },
    {
      "text": "PDDL (Planning Domain Definition Language), standardized in the 1990s, formalizes these for off-the-shelf planners like FF or Optic, outputting action sequences executable by low-level controllers",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "language"
    },
    {
      "text": "This shift from explicit programming to data-driven optimization allows robots to handle complex tasks like navigation and manipulation without predefined trajectories",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "shift"
    },
    {
      "text": "For instance, asynchronous real-world RL frameworks have demonstrated continual improvement in tasks like object grasping, reducing reliance on human demonstrations by learning from physical trials",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "rl"
    },
    {
      "text": "Safety challenges necessitate verifiable guarantees, as AI-driven decisions can exhibit brittleness in edge cases, prompting frameworks like constrained RL to enforce hard limits on actions",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "ai"
    },
    {
      "text": "Mobility and Interaction Ground-Based Locomotion Ground-based locomotion in robotics primarily encompasses wheeled, tracked, and legged systems, each optimized for specific terrains and tasks",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "ground"
    },
    {
      "text": "Wheeled mechanisms dominate due to their mechanical simplicity, high efficiency on flat surfaces, and stability from continuous ground contact",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "wheeled"
    },
    {
      "text": "These approaches address core challenges like energy efficiency, stability, and terrain traversal, with selection driven by environmental demands rather than universality",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "these"
    },
    {
      "text": "However, wheels falter on steep inclines or loose soil , where slip reduces odometry accuracy and risks entrapment",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "however"
    },
    {
      "text": "Systems like the TRX 10-ton unmanned vehicle employ hybrid-electric propulsion for enhanced torque and reduced soil disturbance, supporting military scouting over mud or sand",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "systems"
    },
    {
      "text": "Flexible rubber tracks allow stair climbing and obstacle surmounting up to 0",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "tracks"
    },
    {
      "text": "Flexible rubber tracks allow stair climbing and obstacle surmounting up to 0",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "tracks"
    },
    {
      "text": "Flexible rubber tracks allow stair climbing and obstacle surmounting up to 0",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "tracks"
    },
    {
      "text": "Flexible rubber tracks allow stair climbing and obstacle surmounting up to 0",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "tracks"
    },
    {
      "text": "Flexible rubber tracks allow stair climbing and obstacle surmounting up to 0",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "tracks"
    },
    {
      "text": "5 m, as modeled in simulations showing stable gaits on inclines exceeding 30 degrees",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
      "agent": "5"
    },
    {
      "text": "5 m, as modeled in simulations showing stable gaits on inclines exceeding 30 degrees",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Robotics",
