    },
    {
      "text": "Nitrous oxide (N\u2082O) emissions, primarily from agricultural fertilizer use and industrial processes, have risen by roughly 20%, contributing additional forcing despite lower concentrations",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Climate_change",
      "agent": "primarily"
    },
    {
      "text": "These methods rely on statistical analyses and climate model simulations to compare observed data against ensembles of model runs that isolate individual or combined forcings",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Climate_change",
      "agent": "methods"
    },
    {
      "text": "These early efforts were analytical rather than numerical, relying on simplified energy balance equations without computational simulation of dynamic processes",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Climate_change",
      "agent": "than"
    },
    {
      "text": "The transition to numerical climate models began in the mid-20th century, building on advances in numerical weather prediction (NWP)",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Climate_change",
      "agent": "transition"
    },
    {
      "text": "By the 1960s, GCMs evolved into three-dimensional frameworks incorporating radiative transfer and moist convection",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Climate_change",
      "agent": "gcms"
    },
    {
      "text": "Joseph Smagorinsky's group at NOAA developed operational GCMs starting in 1963, emphasizing realistic simulations of large-scale circulation driven by solar heating gradients",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Climate_change",
      "agent": "s"
    },
    {
      "text": "The 1970s saw the maturation of coupled atmosphere-ocean GCMs (AOGCMs), addressing the limitations of atmosphere-only models that prescribed sea surface temperatures",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Climate_change",
      "agent": "1970s"
    },
    {
      "text": "International coordination via the World Climate Research Programme has driven phases like CMIP6 (circa 2016), incorporating higher-fidelity ocean eddies and ice sheets for millennial-scale projections",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Climate_change",
      "agent": "driven"
    },
    {
      "text": "Discrepancies arise due to model assumptions about feedbacks, such as cloud responses and aerosol effects, which amplify or dampen warming",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Climate_change",
      "agent": "arise"
    },
    {
      "text": "Upper-air temperature trends, measured by satellites since 1979, reveal persistent model overestimation of mid-tropospheric warming rates, exceeding observations by factors of 2-3 in the tropics",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Climate_change",
      "agent": "trends"
    },
    {
      "text": "Beyond temperature, model accuracy varies by variable",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Climate_change",
      "agent": "beyond"
    },
    {
      "text": "Instrumental estimates, which constrain ECS using observed 20th-century warming, radiative forcing, and ocean heat uptake, typically yield lower values",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Climate_change",
      "agent": "instrumental"
    },
