    {
      "text": "5 billion years ago and eukaryotes later developing through endosymbiosis",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cell_(biology)",
      "agent": "later"
    },
    {
      "text": "The structure of a typical cell includes a plasma membrane that acts as a selective barrier, controlling the exchange of materials with the environment",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cell_(biology)",
      "agent": "structure"
    },
    {
      "text": "In eukaryotic cells, the nucleus serves as the control center, directing cellular activities via genetic instructions",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cell_(biology)",
      "agent": "serves"
    },
    {
      "text": "Prokaryotic cells, while lacking these compartments, still carry out analogous functions through simpler mechanisms",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cell_(biology)",
      "agent": "while"
    },
    {
      "text": "Cells perform vital functions that sustain life, including nutrient uptake, energy conversion, protein production , and response to environmental signals",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cell_(biology)",
      "agent": "life"
    },
    {
      "text": "They reproduce via division\u2014binary fission in prokaryotes and mitosis or meiosis in eukaryotes\u2014ensuring continuity and diversity",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cell_(biology)",
      "agent": "mitosis"
    },
    {
      "text": "All living organisms are composed of one or more cells, which serve as the fundamental building blocks for tissues, organs, and entire multicellular bodies",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cell_(biology)",
      "agent": "all"
    },
    {
      "text": "These properties allow cells to maintain homeostasis, adapt to changes, and sustain life processes autonomously or in coordination within multicellular organisms",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cell_(biology)",
      "agent": "properties"
    },
    {
      "text": "Cell sizes vary significantly by type, providing insight into their complexity and function",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cell_(biology)",
      "agent": "cell"
    },
    {
      "text": "Prokaryotic cells, such as bacteria , typically range from 0",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cell_(biology)",
      "agent": "prokaryotic"
    },
    {
      "text": "Prokaryotic cells, such as bacteria , typically range from 0",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cell_(biology)",
      "agent": "prokaryotic"
    },
    {
      "text": "Prokaryotic cells, such as bacteria , typically range from 0",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cell_(biology)",
      "agent": "prokaryotic"
    },
    {
      "text": "Prokaryotic cells, such as bacteria , typically range from 0",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cell_(biology)",
      "agent": "prokaryotic"
    },
    {
      "text": "Prokaryotic cells, such as bacteria , typically range from 0",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cell_(biology)",
      "agent": "prokaryotic"
    },
    {
      "text": "1 to 5 \u03bcm in diameter, reflecting their simpler organization",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cell_(biology)",
      "agent": "1"
