      "text": "29333251(38) MeV/c\u00b2, a difference enabling free neutron beta decay while protons remain stable",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "difference"
    },
    {
      "text": "The negative sign reflects the dominance of down quark contributions in the isovector magnetic moment",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "negative"
    },
    {
      "text": "This observation verified charge conjugation invariance in strong interactions at that energy scale",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "observation"
    },
    {
      "text": "No mass or lifetime discrepancies have been detected, aligning with local quantum field theory requirements",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "mass"
    },
    {
      "text": "CP symmetry tests via antineutron production and decay branching ratios show no violations beyond weak interaction effects, contrasting with observed CP breaking in kaon systems",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "decay"
    },
    {
      "text": "These valence quarks carry the quantum numbers defining the neutron's identity, including its baryon number of +1, isospin of -1/2, and strangeness of 0",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "numbers"
    },
    {
      "text": "High-energy electrons or neutrinos scatter off deuteron targets, allowing extraction of neutron structure functions by subtracting proton contributions from deuterium data",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "targets"
    },
    {
      "text": "The valence quark distribution in the neutron\u2014one up quark and two down quarks\u2014is supported by measurements of structure function moments",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "neutron"
    },
    {
      "text": "These data validate the quark-parton model against alternatives and quantify flavor-specific quark densities within the neutron",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "these"
    },
    {
      "text": "The down quark 's higher mass compared to the up quark contributes to the overall energy balance permitting the decay",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "mass"
    },
    {
      "text": "This ~8-10 second difference corresponds to a 3-5 sigma tension, unresolved despite improvements reducing statistical uncertainties below 0",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "tension"
    },
