      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "reactions"
    },
    {
      "text": "Operating at megawatt beam powers, these pulsed sources (microsecond bursts) provide peak brightness exceeding reactor-based production, supporting high-resolution scattering but demanding extensive infrastructure",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "powers"
    },
    {
      "text": "At nuclear scales, the residual strong force mediates the attraction between neutrons and protons, overcoming electrostatic repulsion to form stable atomic nuclei",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "force"
    },
    {
      "text": "This magnetic coupling , quantified in experiments like Schwinger scattering , allows neutrons to probe material magnetism without charge-based interference",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "magnetic"
    },
    {
      "text": "Detector Technologies Neutron detection relies on indirect methods because neutrons lack electric charge and thus do not ionize matter directly",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "neutron"
    },
    {
      "text": "Instead, detectors exploit nuclear reaction s or scattering events that produce charged particles, gamma rays, or fission fragments detectable by conventional means",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "s"
    },
    {
      "text": "Gas-filled detectors, particularly proportional counters, have historically dominated thermal neutron detection due to their high efficiency and pulse-height discrimination capabilities",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "gas"
    },
    {
      "text": "Scintillation detectors provide versatility for both thermal and fast neutron regimes, often moderated with polyethylene to thermalize incident neutrons",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "scintillation"
    },
    {
      "text": "Recent advances include dual-mode detectors combining 6 Li ^6\\text{Li} 6 Li and 7 Li ^7\\text{Li} 7 Li scintillators for improved neutron-gamma separation",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "advances"
    },
    {
      "text": "Emerging solid-state technologies address limitations in size, ruggedness, and high-temperature operation",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "emerging"
    },
    {
      "text": "Scientific Applications Scattering and Diffraction Studies Neutron scattering encompasses elastic and inelastic processes that reveal structural and dynamical properties of materials at atomic scales",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "applications"
    },
    {
      "text": "This allows precise hydrogen positioning in structures, crucial for hydrogen-bonded systems",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "this"
    },
    {
      "text": "Neutrons' magnetic moment further enables mapping of magnetic structures and spin dynamics, inaccessible via X-rays",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "neutrons"
    },
    {
      "text": "Their weak interaction yields high penetration depths up to centimeters in metals, facilitating bulk rather than surface analysis",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "weak"
    },
    {
      "text": "In materials science , neutron diffraction assesses crystallographic textures via complete pole figures in transmission geometry , aiding analysis of deformed polycrystals",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "neutron"
    },
    {
      "text": "It maps residual stresses in engineering components, correlating lattice strains to applied loads, as demonstrated in studies of welds and turbine blades",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "stresses"
    },
    {
      "text": "Small-angle neutron scattering (SANS) characterizes nanoscale features like precipitates in alloys or polymer morphologies, with resolutions down to 1 nm",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "neutron"
    },
    {
      "text": "In condensed matter, inelastic techniques at facilities like spallation sources measure phonon dispersions to validate lattice dynamics models, informing thermal conductivity predictions",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "condensed"
    },
    {
      "text": "Technological and Energy Applications Nuclear Fission Reactors In nuclear fission reactors, neutrons initiate and sustain the controlled chain reaction that produces heat for electricity generation",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "applications"
    },
    {
      "text": "These prompt neutrons enable the chain reaction, where on average 2",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
