      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "criticality"
    },
    {
      "text": "Graphite-moderated reactors, like the RBMK type, employ carbon as the moderator for similar neutron slowing via elastic scattering",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "graphite"
    },
    {
      "text": "Without moderation, fast neutrons have lower fission efficiency in uranium-235 but can fission plutonium-239 more effectively",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "neutrons"
    },
    {
      "text": "Sodium-cooled fast reactors exemplify this approach, reducing long-lived waste and extending fuel resources, though they represent a smaller fraction of global capacity",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "sodium"
    },
    {
      "text": "Reactor-based facilities offer steady-state operation ideal for certain time-resolved studies, while spallation sources provide superior time-of-flight capabilities for energy-resolved measurements",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "reactor"
    },
    {
      "text": "These facilities operate as user centers, allocating beam time through peer-reviewed proposals to international scientific communities",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "operate"
    },
    {
      "text": "As of 2025, phase II trials continue for unresectable tumors, supported by IAEA-coordinated efforts, though challenges persist in boron delivery uniformity and neutron flux optimization",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "trials"
    },
    {
      "text": "For instance, it detects rare earth elements at microgram levels in small samples, as demonstrated in early experiments with dysprosium",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "samples"
    },
    {
      "text": "Archaeological artifacts benefit from its ability to analyze provenance through minor element profiles, such as in ceramics, without destruction",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "archaeological"
    },
    {
      "text": "Medical applications include in vivo analysis of trace elements like calcium in bones, though limited by radiation exposure concerns",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "applications"
    },
    {
      "text": "On average, fission of uranium-235 or plutonium-239 releases 2 to 3 neutrons, with fast neutrons (energies around 2 MeV) directly inducing further fissions without moderation",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "239"
    },
    {
      "text": "This design relies on the inherent neutron background in uranium but incorporates initiators as additional assurance against fizzle yields",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "neutron"
    },
    {
      "text": "Neutron multiplication proceeds exponentially, with 45 or more generations in 2-3 microseconds yielding about 21 kilotons for Fat Man",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "neutron"
    },
    {
      "text": "Without sufficient neutrons to overcome leakage\u2014minimized in spherical shapes\u2014the reaction fails, underscoring neutrons' causal role in weapon reliability",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "without"
    },
    {
      "text": "Early designs like Urchin , developed at Los Alamos by 1944, addressed timing challenges inherent to implosion's brief supercritical window",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "urchin"
    },
    {
      "text": "The concept originated in the United States in 1958, developed by physicist Samuel T",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "concept"
    },
    {
      "text": "Cohen at Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory as a tactical response to massed armored formations, particularly Soviet tank armies in Europe",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "response"
    },
    {
      "text": "Initial underground tests occurred in 1962, confirming the feasibility of neutron enhancement without proportional increases in blast effects",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "tests"
    },
    {
      "text": "Other nations pursued similar technology; China tested an ERW prototype in 1988 but opted against deployment, citing strategic redundancy and international optics",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Neutron",
      "agent": "nations"
    },
    {
