    {
      "text": "Recent simulations suggest that chondrite parent bodies accreted 2\u20133 million years later, delayed by gaps created by Jupiter's rapid early growth in the protoplanetary disk",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "simulations"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Nice model, proposed by Tsiganis et al",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "model"
    },
    {
      "text": "Furthermore, analyses of asteroidal meteorites in 2024 indicate an early giant planet instability recorded during the protoplanetary disk phase",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "analyses"
    },
    {
      "text": "Current State and Future The Solar System's current dynamics are shaped by gravitational interactions that maintain stability over long timescales",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "dynamics"
    },
    {
      "text": "These forces, combined with planetary gravitational influences, ensure the system's overall stability while allowing subtle evolutions in eccentricities and inclinations",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "forces"
    },
    {
      "text": "Looking ahead, the Solar System's fate is inextricably linked to the Sun's stellar evolution",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "system"
    },
    {
      "text": "Around the resulting white dwarf remnant, surviving outer bodies may maintain roughly Solar System-like orbits, though some scattered planetesimals could be ejected over time",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "around"
    },
    {
      "text": "Potential disruptions from external forces could alter this trajectory earlier",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "potential"
    },
    {
      "text": "These changes carry profound implications for habitability",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "these"
    },
    {
      "text": "By 2 billion years, total water loss could transform Earth into a desert world, well before the red giant phase",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "billion"
    },
    {
      "text": "This dominance underscores the system's hierarchical structure, where the central star captured most of the nebula's material during its formation",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "dominance"
    },
    {
      "text": "Beyond the major planets, the Solar System's small body populations represent remnants of the nebula 's total inventory",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "beyond"
    },
    {
      "text": "These minor reservoirs, though small in mass, provide critical insights into the nebula 's initial chemical diversity and the processes that shaped planetary differentiation",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "mass"
    },
    {
      "text": "Kepler's first law states that planets orbit the Sun in elliptical paths with the Sun at one focus, rather than perfect circles as previously assumed",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "kepler"
    },
    {
      "text": "This inverse-square law unifies terrestrial and celestial mechanics, predicting elliptical orbits under a central inverse-square force",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "mechanics"
    },
    {
      "text": "Orbital elements provide a standardized way to describe the shape, size, and orientation of these elliptical paths relative to a reference plane, typically the ecliptic",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "orbital"
    },
    {
      "text": "These elements evolve slowly due to mutual gravitational influences but remain relatively stable over human timescales",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "these"
    },
    {
      "text": "Gravitational resonances and chaotic dynamics introduce complexities that shape the distribution and stability of orbits",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "gravitational"
    },
    {
      "text": "These effects can lead to chaotic behavior, where small initial differences grow unpredictably, particularly in multi-body systems",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "these"
    },
    {
      "text": "The long-term stability of orbits depends on hierarchical structures and bounded chaos",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Solar_System",
      "agent": "term"
    },
    {
      "text": "This distance highlights the gradual expansion of explored space, with Voyager 1 traveling at about 3",
