    },
    {
      "text": "9 for simple GET requests without headers or status codes, prioritizing minimalism to encourage rapid prototyping and interoperability",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_Wide_Web",
      "agent": "9"
    },
    {
      "text": "These early documents outlined HTML as a tag-based language for structuring content and HTTP as a stateless request-response protocol, though lacking formal ratification",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_Wide_Web",
      "agent": "documents"
    },
    {
      "text": "0 followed in 1996 via RFC 1945, introducing methods like POST and basic authentication, reflecting lessons from early deployments",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_Wide_Web",
      "agent": "0"
    },
    {
      "text": "0 followed in 1996 via RFC 1945, introducing methods like POST and basic authentication, reflecting lessons from early deployments",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_Wide_Web",
      "agent": "0"
    },
    {
      "text": "0 followed in 1996 via RFC 1945, introducing methods like POST and basic authentication, reflecting lessons from early deployments",
      "source": "Grokipedia-World_Wide_Web",
