    {
      "text": "Unsubsidized LCOE estimates for new nuclear builds vary by project specifics and regional factors; Lazard 's 2025 analysis, drawing from U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "lcoe"
    },
    {
      "text": "Long-Term Viability and Market Incentives Nuclear power's long-term viability hinges on sustained fuel availability, technological advancements, and operational reliability",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "term"
    },
    {
      "text": "Existing plants demonstrate exceptional capacity factor s exceeding 92%, far surpassing natural gas or renewables, enabling consistent output over decades-long lifespans",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "s"
    },
    {
      "text": "Economically, nuclear's upfront capital intensity \u2014evident in recent U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "nuclear"
    },
    {
      "text": "Economically, nuclear's upfront capital intensity \u2014evident in recent U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "nuclear"
    },
    {
      "text": "Economically, nuclear's upfront capital intensity \u2014evident in recent U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "nuclear"
    },
    {
      "text": "Economically, nuclear's upfront capital intensity \u2014evident in recent U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "nuclear"
    },
    {
      "text": "Economically, nuclear's upfront capital intensity \u2014evident in recent U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "nuclear"
    },
    {
      "text": "Market incentives currently disadvantage nuclear relative to subsidized renewables, with U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "relative"
    },
    {
      "text": "Market incentives currently disadvantage nuclear relative to subsidized renewables, with U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "relative"
    },
    {
      "text": "Market incentives currently disadvantage nuclear relative to subsidized renewables, with U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "relative"
    },
    {
      "text": "Market incentives currently disadvantage nuclear relative to subsidized renewables, with U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "relative"
    },
    {
      "text": "Market incentives currently disadvantage nuclear relative to subsidized renewables, with U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "relative"
    },
    {
      "text": "The nuclear production tax credit lacks inflation adjustment, unlike renewable counterparts, distorting competition by underpricing intermittent sources that require backup infrastructure",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "unlike"
    },
    {
      "text": "Carbon pricing or reformed regulations could align incentives with nuclear's dispatchable, low-emission attributes, fostering viability amid projected energy demand surges from population and economic growth",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "carbon"
    },
    {
      "text": "No injuries or health effects occurred; over a dozen post-accident epidemiological studies, including those by the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "effects"
    },
    {
      "text": "No injuries or health effects occurred; over a dozen post-accident epidemiological studies, including those by the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "effects"
    },
    {
      "text": "No injuries or health effects occurred; over a dozen post-accident epidemiological studies, including those by the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "effects"
    },
    {
      "text": "No injuries or health effects occurred; over a dozen post-accident epidemiological studies, including those by the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "effects"
    },
    {
      "text": "No injuries or health effects occurred; over a dozen post-accident epidemiological studies, including those by the U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "effects"
    },
    {
      "text": "Nuclear Regulatory Commission (NRC) and independent researchers, confirmed zero discernible direct health impacts on workers or residents, despite initial public evacuations influenced by uncertainty",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "researchers"
    },
    {
      "text": "Post-accident fortifications, such as elevated seawalls and diversified power supplies, reflect causal lessons from natural disaster vulnerabilities rather than fission processes",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "fortifications"
    },
    {
      "text": "Nuclear Regulatory Commission standards mandate CDF below 10^{-4} for licensed plants, with Generation III+ designs targeting 10^{-5} or lower through passive safety features and redundancy",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "regulatory"
    },
    {
      "text": "Pre-Fukushima PRAs for that site estimated 10^{-6} annually, underscoring underestimation of external hazards like tsunamis but affirming low baseline risks from internal events",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "pre"
    },
    {
      "text": "These metrics, informed by fault and event tree modeling, emphasize causal pathways from initiating events to outcomes, prioritizing empirical validation over worst-case speculation",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "metrics"
    },
    {
      "text": "Nuclear Regulatory Commission (NRC) and the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA)",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "nuclear"
    },
    {
      "text": "Monitoring ensures adherence to standards through personal , area, and environmental systems",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "monitoring"
    },
    {
      "text": "Operational emissions remain below 1 g CO2eq/kWh, excluding indirect grid effects",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "remain"
    },
    {
      "text": "On biodiversity, nuclear's small footprint minimizes direct ecosystem disruption, with plant sites often sited on previously industrialized land and fenced perimeters enabling adjacent habitat continuity",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "footprint"
    },
    {
      "text": "Initial management involves interim storage to allow decay of short-lived isotopes and heat dissipation, followed by either reprocessing to recover usable materials or direct disposal",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "management"
    },
    {
      "text": "In the United States, political delays have stalled Yucca Mountain despite technical viability, leaving reliance on extended interim storage, though consent-based siting discussions continue",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "united"
    },
    {
      "text": "These strategies ensure containment, with modeling and natural analogs confirming negligible biosphere impact post-closure",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "containment"
    },
    {
      "text": "Vitrification immobilizes certain HLW forms, such as defense wastes, into durable glass logs for repository emplacement, further reducing leachability",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "vitrification"
    },
    {
      "text": "Iraq under Saddam Hussein pursued enrichment via electromagnetic isotope separation in the 1980s alongside its Osirak power reactor plans, though international intervention halted progress",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "hussein"
    },
    {
      "text": "Sabotage risks include insider threats and cyber intrusions, though successful disruptions of reactor operations remain rare",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "risks"
    },
    {
      "text": "RPS, particularly radioisotope thermoelectric generators (RTGs), harness the decay heat from plutonium-238 (Pu-238) to produce electricity through thermoelectric conversion, offering longevity without moving parts",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "particularly"
    },
    {
      "text": "RTGs have powered over two dozen U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "rtgs"
    },
    {
      "text": "RTGs have powered over two dozen U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "rtgs"
    },
    {
      "text": "RTGs have powered over two dozen U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "rtgs"
    },
    {
      "text": "RTGs have powered over two dozen U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "rtgs"
    },
    {
      "text": "RTGs have powered over two dozen U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "rtgs"
    },
    {
      "text": "The SNAP program, active from 1955 to 1973, developed compact reactors using enriched uranium fuel, but subsequent U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
      "agent": "snap"
    },
    {
      "text": "The SNAP program, active from 1955 to 1973, developed compact reactors using enriched uranium fuel, but subsequent U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Nuclear_power",
