      "agent": "key"
    },
    {
      "text": "Missile sites remained exposed without camouflage or dispersal sufficient to withstand concentrated U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cuban_Missile_Crisis",
      "agent": "sites"
    },
    {
      "text": "Overall, Soviet deployments projected parity but masked an inferior position, with forces outnumbered locally by U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cuban_Missile_Crisis",
      "agent": "deployments"
    },
    {
      "text": "Similarly, Italy hosted Jupiter deployments starting in 1961, positioned to threaten Soviet territory and integrated into NATO's forward-based nuclear posture",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cuban_Missile_Crisis",
      "agent": "similarly"
    },
    {
      "text": "These land-based, liquid-fueled missiles were vulnerable to preemptive strikes and increasingly obsolete compared to submarine-launched Polaris systems, prompting U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cuban_Missile_Crisis",
      "agent": "land"
    },
    {
      "text": "During the Cuban Missile Crisis, Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev cited the Jupiter missiles in Turkey and Italy as a provocation equivalent to U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cuban_Missile_Crisis",
      "agent": "crisis"
    },
    {
      "text": "-Soviet bargain, announced publicly on October 28, 1962, committed the Soviets to withdrawing missiles from Cuba in exchange for a U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cuban_Missile_Crisis",
      "agent": "bargain"
    },
    {
      "text": "Italy's Jupiters were also slated for quiet withdrawal as part of the broader U",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cuban_Missile_Crisis",
      "agent": "jupiters"
    },
    {
      "text": "This secrecy aimed to avoid perceptions of linkage that could undermine NATO unity or signal capitulation to Soviet pressure",
      "source": "Grokipedia-Cuban_Missile_Crisis",
      "agent": "secrecy"
    },
    {
      "text": "Allied reactions highlighted tensions over nuclear basing and deterrence credibility",
